{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1678 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "28923b2e7bf395b173f89a81350a2ffd38d98151b3428f4c4cf50fb42e066903" :edb/db-before-t 1677 :edb/db-after-t 1678 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534990 50 #inst "2026-10-08T13:27:32.344Z" 13194139534990 true] [17592186133616 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j} - 2z\\,\\mathbf{k}$. Evaluate $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $\\left(-1,0,2\\right)$." 13194139534990 false] [17592186133616 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j} - 2z\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ Evaluate $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}$ at the point  $\\left(-1,0,2\\right).$" 13194139534990 true] [17592186133616 1145 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}+R\\,\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ncurlF &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ y & x^{2} & -2z\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2z) - \\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2})]i - [\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2z) - \\frac{∂}{∂z}(y)]j + [\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}) - \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)]k \\\\\n&= (0 - 0)i - (0 - 0)j + (2x - 1)k \\\\\n&= (2x - 1)k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm {curl}\\, \\mathbf{F}$ at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(-1,0,2\\right)\n$$\n\nand obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(curlF)(-1, 0, 2) &= (2(-1) - 1)k =-3k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534990 false] [17592186133616 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\,\\mathbf{i}+Q\\,\\mathbf{j}+R\\,\\mathbf{k}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the curl of $\\mathbf F$ is the vector field given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/888b12c4f687dba1199a82d7e3a5e6897e7599ea812753423de5a23ffd756888.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ y & {x}^{2} & -2z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2})]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2z)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y)]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-0)j+(2x-1)k \\\\  & =(2x-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\textrm {curl}\\, \\mathbf{F}$ at the point $\\left(-1,0,2\\right),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\text{curl}\\,F)(-1,0,2) & =(2(-1)-1)\\,k=-3\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534990 true] [17592186133620 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/71c58bf1b0fe4259ac50633a457f6a76639c0a0c188b66cce5e0fc3c7e5b0329.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational\n\n- $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." 13194139534990 true] [17592186133620 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$, shown above. Given that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2132/Source/Images/a7e6add34274406286967ea9c1d41886.png)\n\n1. $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$\n2. $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$\n3. $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational\n4. $\\textrm{curl}\\, \\mathbf F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." 13194139534990 false] [17592186133620 1145 "Recall that $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf F$.\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$.\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2132/Source/Images/83eaadb4dc59f6f9352c6d5c3d9901b6.png)\n\nIf\n\n$$\n\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0\n$$\n\neverywhere in $\\mathbb R^3$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, whereas statement II is true. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$.\n- Statements III is false. Since $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0$, the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is not irrotational.\n- Statement IV is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point upward. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ points upward, in the direction of increasing $z$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"" 13194139534990 false] [17592186133620 1145 "Recall that $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F =  \\mathbf 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/86aee33cdfa96ac0a61468cc9657e791e399f31c024cdbc3a932193dc897d534.png)\n\nIf $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F = \\mathbf 0$ everywhere in $\\mathbb R^3$, we say that $\\mathbf F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, whereas statement II is true. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0.$\n\n- Statements III is false. Since $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ the vector field $\\mathbf F$ is not irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point upward. Therefore, $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ points upward, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\"" 13194139534990 true] [17592186133637 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^{x+y} \\, \\mathbf{i} + 3xy \\, \\mathbf{j}$. Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-1,1)$." 13194139534990 false] [17592186133637 1100 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^{x+y} \\, \\mathbf{i} + 3xy \\, \\mathbf{j}.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ at the point $(-1,1).$" 13194139534990 true] [17592186133637 1145 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nis a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy) - \\frac{∂}{∂y}(e^{x + y}) \\\\\n&= 3y - e^{x + y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-1,1)$, and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})_{(-1, 1)} &= 3(1) - e^{-1 + 1} \\\\\n&= 3 - e^{0} \\\\\n&= 3 - 1 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534990 false] [17592186133637 1145 "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}) \\\\  & =3y-{e}^{x+y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(-1,1),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(-1,1)} & =3(1)-{e}^{-1+1} \\\\  & =3-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534990 true] [17592186133800 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325254 13194139534990 true] [17592186133800 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325268 13194139534990 true] [17592186133800 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325310 13194139534990 true] [17592186133800 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325317 13194139534990 true] [17592186133804 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325233 13194139534990 true] [17592186133804 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325240 13194139534990 true] [17592186133804 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325247 13194139534990 true] [17592186133804 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325261 13194139534990 true] [17592186133821 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325275 13194139534990 true] [17592186133821 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325282 13194139534990 true] [17592186133821 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325289 13194139534990 true] [17592186133821 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325296 13194139534990 true] [17592186133821 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325303 13194139534990 true] [17592186133984 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534990 true] [17592186133984 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/38a7ae83a66a2c1ff6a012b9405c6621b0c0a162018065549e75a1d08bc04d44.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." 13194139534990 true] [17592186133984 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2132/Source/Images/q-105092.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y, z)$, shown above. Given that $F(x, y, z)$ is independent of $z$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $curlF = 0$\n2. $curlF \\ne 0$\n3. $F$ is irrotational\n4. $curlF$ points in the negative $z$-direction." 13194139534990 false] [17592186133984 1145 "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534990 true] [17592186325233 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325234 13194139534990 true] [17592186325233 1089 "q-105667" 13194139534990 true] [17592186325233 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534990 true] [17592186325233 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/773ff9d880deed92f8034d6949cfee85edbcdf735c8e125f1ec5762137fadb9c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." 13194139534990 true] [17592186325233 1113 #uuid "bd27acb9-d270-5bdc-978c-5e2351e38d23" 13194139534990 true] [17592186325233 1145 "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045767/assets/4e344a0a1f6b0c535c5